window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-08","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-08-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-01/","title_he":"א – הפירות החייבים בתרומות ומעשרות","title_en":"Produce Obligated in Terumot and Ma'asrot","body_en":"<p>According to most Rishonim, only the five species of grain, wine and oil, which are the important foods for a person, are obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot by Torah law, as it is stated (Devarim 18:4): \"The first of your grain, your wine and your oil\" (Rashi, Tosafot, Rash, Rashba, Ran and Ritva). From grain the basic food is prepared, such as bread and mezonot (grain-based foods); wine gladdens and nourishes; and olive oil is very nourishing and enriches flavors. In the days of King Ḥizkiya, they added an enactment to separate terumot u-ma'asrot also from other tree fruits and from the various legumes and vegetables.</p><p>Some say that legumes and tree fruits are also obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot by Torah law, as it is stated regarding ma'aser sheni (Vayikra 27:30): \"And all the tithe of the land, whether of the seed of the land or of the fruit of the tree, is the Lord's…\" And it is also stated (Devarim 14:22): \"You shall surely tithe all the produce of your seed that the field brings forth year by year\" (Yerushalmi Ma'asrot 1:1; Rambam; Rash Sirilio). Although the Torah explicitly mentioned three species, its intention was to teach us the general rule through them. From grain we learn that all kinds of legumes are also obligated, and from grapes and olives we learn that all tree fruits are obligated. And the Sages added an enactment to separate terumot u-ma'asrot also from all kinds of vegetables.</p><p>But whatever does not grow from the ground, such as milk and meat, is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. Although animals feed on grass, meat and milk are not considered produce of the field but rather secondary growths. Likewise, mushrooms are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, since they do not grow from the soil. Likewise, plant species that people do not usually eat, but are animal fodder, or are intended to season human food but are not eaten by themselves, are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (as explained in the next halakha).<sup>1</sup></p><p>By Torah law, a person must separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce that he picks and gathers for his household's food, as it is stated (Devarim 14:22-23): \"You shall surely tithe all the produce of your seed… and you shall eat.\" And by rabbinic law, one is obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot also from produce that a person picks in order to sell. Produce whose processing has not been completed, such as grapes to make wine from them or olives to make oil from them – if the buyer makes wine and oil from them for his household's use, they become obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot by Torah law, and if in order to sell them, by rabbinic law (Bava Metzi'a 88b; Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 2:1-2).</p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"Regarding teruma, grain, wine and oil are stated (Bamidbar 18:12; Devarim 18:3-4). By contrast, regarding ma'aser sheni it is stated (Vayikra 27:30): \"And all the tithe of the land, whether of the seed of the land or of the fruit of the tree, is the Lord's…\" And in Devarim (14:22-23) it first states: \"You shall surely tithe all the produce of your seed that the field brings forth…\" and afterwards states: \"the tithe of your grain, your wine and your oil.\" According to most poskim, the obligation of terumot u-ma'asrot by Torah law applies only to grain, wine and oil, and what the Torah left unspecified regarding ma'aser sheni is meant according to what we learned regarding teruma; and so it is implied by the Bavli (Bekhorot 54a). This is the opinion of Rashi, Tosafot, Rash, Rid, Ri'az, Rosh, Rashba, Ran, Ritva, Ra'ah, Meiri and others. Some hold that only wine and oil are obligated by Torah law, but not grapes and olives (Talmidei Rabbeinu Yona, Riva and others). And some say that all seven species are obligated by Torah law (Ritz Gi'at). It is explained in Nedarim (55a) that in the days of Ḥizkiya they enacted that terumot u-ma'asrot be separated from other fruits and vegetables (Rosh there; Rash Ma'asrot 1:1).<br>On the other hand, in the Yerushalmi Ma'asrot 1:1 it is explained that all tree fruits and legumes are obligated by Torah law, as stated regarding ma'aser sheni, and the grain, wine and oil mentioned in the verses are only particulars that teach about the general rule; only vegetables were excluded from the rule and are obligated only by rabbinic law. And so wrote the Rambam (Hilkhot Terumot 2:1; 6). One can say that the main difference between vegetables and legumes is that vegetables are less nourishing, and the main difference between vegetables and tree fruits is that vegetables are less gladdening. And some say that by Torah law, for terumot and ma'aser rishon only grain, wine and oil are obligated, while for ma'aser sheni all species are obligated, including vegetables (Yere'im and Semag)."}]},{"slug":"17-08-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-02/","title_he":"ב – פרטי הדינים","title_en":"Details of the Laws","body_en":"<p>Any human food that grows from the land is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, whether it is eaten in its natural form, or mashed, or made into juice. But parts of the fruit that are not considered human food are exempt. Therefore, peels and seeds that are not fit to be eaten are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, and those that are fit to be eaten, such as apple peel and watermelon seeds, are obligated. Grape leaves picked for food, as a wrapping for rice and meat, are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, since they are generally not considered food.</p><p>Spices that serve only to give flavor, fragrance or color, such as black pepper, cinnamon, cumin, coffee beans and tea leaves, are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, since they are not eaten by themselves. But parsley and coriander leaves, which are usually mixed into salad, are obligated, since they are recognizable in their form in the salad and consequently are considered to be eaten by themselves. One who cooks soup with parsley, garlic and onion, even though his intention is that they only give flavor and afterwards he throws them away – the soup is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, since its flavor comes from vegetables that are usually eaten in their own form as well.<sup>2</sup></p><p>Plants intended for medicinal use, fragrance or decoration are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. Likewise, khat leaves are exempt. One who grows seeds that are not fit to be eaten in order to grow vegetables, such as onion seeds, they are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. One who takes seeds and sprouts them need not tithe the sprouts. Although some are stringent, the main view is that of the lenient.<sup>3</sup></p><p>Species that are eaten by animals, even if there is a person who wishes to eat them and sowed them for his own consumption, since they are animal fodder, are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. But species that are sometimes eaten by people and sometimes by animals, such as barley – if he sowed and gathered them for people, they are obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, and if he sowed and gathered them for animals, they are exempt (see Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 2:2; Ma'adanei Eretz, Terumot 2:7).<sup>4</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"So wrote Tosafot (Yoma 81b, s.v. \"She-ha-pilpelim\") and the Rosh, in the first explanation; and so wrote Tosafot Yeshanim, Rashba and Ran. Regarding tea leaves, some are stringent to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from them without a blessing (Rav Eliyahu, Ha-Torah Ve-ha-aretz vol. 4, p. 10). However, since the vast majority of poskim are lenient, and the basis of the law is rabbinic, the halakha follows the lenient view. <b>Mint leaves</b>, with which many customarily flavor a drink, and which some also eat as part of a salad: if one planted them for eating, they are obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, and if only for flavoring, they are exempt; and in case of doubt, one should separate without a blessing.<br>Regarding sugar made from cane, by squeezing out its juices and cooking them, some say it is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, because the canes are not a fruit (Responsa Radbaz 1:563; Nehar Mitzrayim and others). And sugar made from sugar beet is also exempt, because this beet is not fit to be eaten (Har Tzvi, Zera'im 1:79; Ḥazon Ovadia, Terumot u-Ma'asrot p. 111). On the other hand, some say that cane sugar is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot (Kaftor Va-feraḥ 26), and some say that even if cane sugar is exempt, beet sugar is obligated (Minḥat Shlomo 3:150:3; Mishpetei Eretz 1:9). In practice, since many poskim were lenient and this was the custom, and the dispute concerns a rabbinic law, the halakha follows the lenient.<br><b>Orange peels</b>: Some say they are obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, because sweets are made from the whole peel and many people eat the white peel as is, and it is even permitted ab initio nowadays to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from the peels on behalf of the main fruit (Tzitz Eliezer 1:1; 3:21). Mishpetei Eretz (8, n. 42) inclines to permit this in a pressing situation. On the other hand, according to the Ḥazon Ish (Ma'asrot 1:30), the peels are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot since only a minority of them are intended for eating. And some say that although one who wishes to eat peels must separate terumot u-ma'asrot, one does not separate from them on behalf of the main fruit (see Ha-ma'aser Ve-ha-teruma 3, n. 38). And so it seems in practice. One who simply separates terumot u-ma'asrot from oranges has also exempted the peels."},{"n":3,"en":"Some are stringent to require terumot u-ma'asrot from sprouts, even though one has already separated from the seeds themselves, since his intention is to grow the vegetable that comes up from them (Rav Eliyahu, Ha-Torah Ve-ha-aretz vol. 3, p. 215). In Mishpetei Eretz (1:14) he was stringent to separate terumot u-ma'asrot without a blessing from what grows in water, and lenient regarding sprouts grown in moisture, because they are similar to mushrooms. However, in practice, for several reasons one may be lenient even if they grew in water. First, for two reasons the discussion concerns rabbinic law: <b>a)</b> They grow in a closed vessel, whose law is like that of a pot without a hole. <b>b)</b> Their law is like that of a vegetable. And for three reasons there is a dispute whether there is any obligation regarding them:<br><b>a)</b> Some hold that what grows in water is exempt from Shevi'it and terumot u-ma'asrot (below, note 17). <b>b)</b> There is a dispute whether what grows in a house is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot by rabbinic law: according to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'aser 1:10) it is obligated, and according to the Ra'avad it is exempt. <b>c)</b> There is room for doubt whether perhaps the stalk is secondary to the seed, for all its material comes from the seed, from which terumot u-ma'asrot have already been separated. And this is the opinion of Rav Ariel (Be-ohala Shel Torah 4:47). Were it easy to be stringent in this, there might be room to say that it is proper to be meticulous in order to satisfy all the poskim; but since it involves great trouble, as well as doubts – for if one separates for everything today, by tomorrow they will have grown more, and a doubt will arise whether one must separate for what grew further and how – it is therefore correct to rule according to the essential law, that in a doubt concerning a rabbinic law one is lenient."},{"n":4,"en":"Regarding karshinin (vetch), which is animal fodder and in time of famine human food, the Sages decreed that it is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot (Yerushalmi Ḥalla 4:4), even when it was sown for animals (Mahari Korkos and Radbaz)."}]},{"slug":"17-08-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-03/","title_he":"ג – הפקר","title_en":"Hefker","body_en":"<p>Hefker (ownerless) produce, that is, produce that any person may take, is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, for it is stated (Devarim 14:29): \"And the Levite, because he has no portion or inheritance with you, shall come\" – specifically from produce in which he has no portion or inheritance must one separate terumot u-ma'asrot, but from hefker produce, since he does have a portion and inheritance in it, one does not separate terumot u-ma'asrot (Yerushalmi Terumot 1:3; Ma'asrot 1:1). Therefore, hikers who picked fruit growing wild in a place that belongs to no one may eat it in the field or at home without separating terumot u-ma'asrot from it.</p><p>Fruit trees planted for decoration in public gardens that belong to the municipality or to a public association – as long as those responsible for the gardens allow anyone to pick the fruit without restriction, the fruit is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. And if the matter is not clear, they should inform three people that the fruit is hefker, and thereby the fruit will be exempt without doubt.</p><p>One who wishes to declare his produce hefker must say before three people: \"My produce is hefker,\" and thereby it becomes hefker, and anyone may acquire it. At least two of the listeners must be fit to testify, so that if the third comes to acquire the produce, the two can testify that he acquired it lawfully (SA ḤM 273:7). If he declared the produce hefker for the poor and not for the rich, or even declared it hefker for everyone except one person, it is not hefker and one must separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it (Mishna Pe'a 6:1).</p><p>The hefker that exempts produce from tithes is from the time it reached \"onat ha-ma'asrot\" (the tithing stage), that is, it became fit to be eaten in a pressing situation (below, halakha 6), until it was picked and became \"fixed for tithes\" (below, halakha 7). If he declared his produce hefker while it was still attached to the trees, but not his field, the produce is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 1:12). But if he declared the field hefker and not the produce, the produce is obligated. Likewise, one who sows a hefker field – even though the field remains hefker, since the produce is his, the produce is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 2:11).</p><p>If he declared his produce hefker, even for one hour, anyone who acquired it is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, and the owner of the produce cannot retract the hefker after another person has acquired it. But if no other person acquired it, the Sages enacted that as long as three days have not passed since the hefker, the owner of the produce may retract the hefker and annul it, or acquire the produce by lifting it, and the produce will be obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. Only if the produce remains ownerless for three days, and afterwards he goes back and acquires it, will he be exempt from separating terumot u-ma'asrot from it. The reason for the enactment is to block swindlers who would declare their produce hefker for a short while and immediately reacquire it, and in truth did not intend to make it ownerless but only to exempt themselves from the obligation of terumot u-ma'asrot.<sup>5</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"<b>The time of the hefker</b>: If he declared it hefker before it reached onat ha-ma'asrot, and another person or he himself acquired it again before it reached onat ha-ma'asrot, since it was not hefker from the time it reached onat ha-ma'asrot, it became obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. Likewise, if he declared it hefker after it was fixed for tithes, the obligation of tithes remains upon it. Although hefker is effective in exempting from terumot u-ma'asrot from when it reached onat ha-ma'asrot until it became fixed for tithes, the Sages forbade declaring the produce hefker in order to exempt it from terumot u-ma'asrot from the time its processing was completed, even though it had not yet become fixed for tithes. But if he transgressed and declared it hefker, since it had not yet become fixed for tithes, it is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 3:20; Shakh YD 331:116).<br>Nedarim 43b-44b, following the opinion of the Rambam (Hilkhot Nedarim 2:17), as explained by the Kesef Mishneh, and so it was ruled in SA (ḤM 273:9). Some say that only when he retracted the hefker within three days is he obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, but if he acquired the produce by an act of acquisition, in the way other people can acquire it, he is exempt. And the enactment is still effective against swindlers, for since the owner of the produce must acquire the produce by picking all of it or lifting it, he fears that before he manages to acquire all the produce, others will come and pick from it; therefore only if he annuls the hefker retroactively within three days does the produce return to his possession, and the produce also returns to its obligation of terumot u-ma'asrot as at first (Rashba and Rosh, Nedarim 43b; Leḥem Mishneh on the Rambam's view; Netivot Ha-mishpat 273:2). And some say that the enactment was that anyone who acquires this produce in the first three days, whether the owner of the field or other people, is obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it, and only one who acquires it after three days is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Ran and Tosafot, Nedarim 43b).<br>If he picked a very large amount of produce from hefker, to the point that he made a pile on the threshing floor from it, since he appears to be the owner, the Sages obligated him to separate terumot u-ma'asrot because of mar'it ayin (appearances) (Berakhot 40b; Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 2:9)."}]},{"slug":"17-08-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-04/","title_he":"ד – נטע רבעי, שביעית ומתנות עניים","title_en":"Neta Reva'i, Shevi'it and Gifts to the Poor","body_en":"<p>The fruit of <b>neta reva'i</b> is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. As is known, the fruit that grows in the first three years of a tree is forbidden under the law of orla, and the fruit of the fourth year is neta reva'i fruit; it is holy, and it is a mitzva to eat it in purity in Jerusalem (above 2:9). Since it is consecrated to its mitzva, one does not leave leket, shikheḥa and pe'a from it for the poor, and one does not separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser Sheni Ve-neta Reva'i 9:4). Fruit that is possibly neta reva'i and possibly of the fifth year – one separates terumot u-ma'asrot from it without a blessing, but there is no obligation to give the ma'aser rishon to the Levite and the ma'aser ani to the poor person, since these are monetary obligations, and as with every monetary obligation, without clear proof that one is indeed obligated to pay this debt, there is no obligation to pay it (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 13:20-21).</p><p>Produce that grows in the <b>seventh</b> year is hefker and exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot.<sup>6</sup></p><p>Produce of leket, shikheḥa, pe'a, peret and olelot (gleanings, forgotten sheaves, the corner of the field, fallen grapes and underdeveloped grape clusters) that must be left for the poor is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot.<sup>7</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"If the owner of the field transgressed the commandments of the Torah and forbade people to enter his field, and picked the seventh-year produce for himself, some say he must separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it (based on Beit Yosef), and some say he is exempt (Mabit). There is likewise a dispute regarding produce that grew in a gentile's field in the seventh year, which the gentile sold to a Jew who gathered it from the field. According to the Mabit, the law of Shevi'it applies to the produce, and it is holy and exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. According to Rabbi Yosef Karo, the law of Shevi'it does not apply to gentiles' produce, and since a Jew gathered it, and the obligation of terumot u-ma'asrot takes effect at the time of gathering, he must separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it. In practice, one should separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it without a blessing, and since ma'aser rishon and ma'aser ani are monetary and there is doubt about their obligation, there is no need to give them. According to Rabbi Yosef Karo one should separate ma'aser ani from it, and some were concerned to separate ma'aser sheni as well, and so wrote Rav Kook, that this is proper ab initio (Mishpat Kohen 82; Da'at Kohen 239; Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it 3:2; 5:11).<br>The determining time for the tithing year for trees in all years is Tu Bi-Shvat; however, in the seventh year the determining time is 1 Tishrei, so that whatever reached ḥanata within the seventh year, from 1 Tishrei until 29 Elul, has the law of Shevi'it and is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it 3, n. 6)."},{"n":7,"en":"It is stated (Devarim 14:29): \"And the Levite, because he has no portion or inheritance with you, shall come\"; but gifts to the poor, in which the Levite, if he is poor, has a portion, are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Yerushalmi Terumot 1:3; Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 2:9). In practice, it is not worthwhile today for the poor to come to the fields and gather their gifts, and therefore they belong to the owner of the field and consequently are obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot – unless the owner of the field knows that there are poor people who are interested in gathering the gifts in his field, in which case they are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Ritva, Gittin 47a; above 6:9)."}]},{"slug":"17-08-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-05/","title_he":"ה – עונת המעשרות וחשבון השנים","title_en":"Onat Ha-ma'asrot and the Reckoning of the Years","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of separating terumot u-ma'asrot applies only after the completion of the work of gathering the produce and its becoming fixed for tithes (below halakhot 6-7). However, \"onat ha-ma'asrot\" (the tithing stage), according to which the tithing year is determined, is fixed by the time the produce emerges from the unripe stage and reaches the stage at which it can be eaten in a pressing situation. This is the time called \"hevi'u shelish\" (reaching a third) in grain and legumes, that is, they have reached a third of their ripening and have become fit to be eaten in a pressing situation; and it is the time of ḥanata in tree fruit. A sign of onat ha-ma'asrot is that from that stage, if one sows the grain or the legumes or the seeds in the fruit, they will sprout.<sup>8</sup></p><p>Onat ha-ma'asrot determines the tithing year for tree fruit, grain and legumes (but not vegetables). Produce that reached onat ha-ma'asrot before its Rosh Ha-shana belongs to the previous year; after its Rosh Ha-shana, to the following year. Two fundamental halakhot depend on this.</p><p>The first: one must separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce of the same year, as it is stated (Devarim 14:22): \"You shall surely tithe all the produce of your seed that the field brings forth year by year\" – \"this teaches that one does not tithe from one year on behalf of another\" (Sifrei there). And if he separated from the produce of one year on behalf of another, his actions are void (Mishna Terumot 1:5; Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 5:11).</p><p>The second: in years 1, 2, 4 and 5 of the Shemita cycle one separates ma'aser sheni, and in years 3 and 6 one separates ma'aser ani, and the time that determines the year with regard to the produce is onat ha-ma'asrot.</p><p>The Rosh Ha-shana for grain and legumes is 1 Tishrei. Whatever reached onat ha-ma'asrot before 1 Tishrei belongs to the previous year, and whatever reached onat ha-ma'asrot after 1 Tishrei belongs to the following year.</p><p>The Rosh Ha-shana for tree fruit is postponed by four and a half months to Tu Bi-Shvat, because any fruit that reached ḥanata before Tu Bi-Shvat, such as citrus fruit, is known to have grown by virtue of the rains of the past year, and therefore its law is that of the past year. And if it reached ḥanata after Tu Bi-Shvat, like most fruit, its law is that of the new year, since it grew by virtue of this year's rains. However, in the seventh year, the year for trees begins on 1 Tishrei, so that whatever reached ḥanata from 1 Tishrei until Tu Bi-Shvat of the seventh year is hefker and exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, and whatever reached ḥanata from 1 Tishrei until Tu Bi-Shvat in the year after the seventh has the law of the first year (Pe'at Ha-shulḥan 22, n. 14; Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it 3, n. 6).</p><p>For vegetables the law is different. The Rosh Ha-shana for vegetables is 1 Tishrei, as for grain and legumes, but their onat ha-ma'asrot is at the time of their harvesting, and not \"from when they reached a third.\" This is because they grow through irrigation, and consequently can be grown in all seasons of the year; therefore it is not their growth that determines, but their harvesting. In addition, they do not ripen all at once, so that if the year were determined by the stage at which the produce is fit to be eaten in a pressing situation, it is possible that within one field some of the vegetables would belong to the previous year and some to the following year, without its being possible to distinguish between them. Therefore the Sages established that their tithing year is determined by the time of their harvesting, which is a clear time. And the same law applies to rice, millet and sesame (Rosh Ha-shana 14a).</p><p>The etrog, too, like vegetables, grows on all waters, for when it is irrigated it continues to grow beyond its natural season, and therefore its tithing year is determined by the time it is picked. However, since it is a tree, its Rosh Ha-shana is on Tu Bi-Shvat. But the law of other citrus fruits is like that of other trees, whose year is determined by ḥanata, since they have a fixed season in which they grow and are picked.<sup>9</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"The Sages expounded: \"'You shall surely tithe all the produce of your seed' – something that is sown and sprouts\" (Devarim 14:22; Yerushalmi Ma'asrot 1:2). The Sages gave signs for various fruits as to when their onat ha-ma'asrot is (Mishna Ma'asrot 1:1-4; Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 2:3-5). However, the basic rule for all produce is that whatever is fit to be eaten in a pressing situation has reached onat ha-ma'asrot, as explained in Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it 3, n. 6. Produce that was picked before it reached onat ha-ma'asrot, even if a person succeeds in making it fit for his eating, will never become obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot."},{"n":9,"en":"These laws are explained in Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser Sheni 1:2-10. The difference between legumes, regarding which we follow onat ha-ma'asrot, and vegetables, regarding which we follow the harvesting, is that in legumes the seed is the food, whereas in vegetables the food is the leaves, such as lettuce and cabbage, or a fruit that has seeds inside it but whose essence is not the seed, such as zucchini and tomatoes. According to Tosafot (Rosh Ha-shana 13b, s.v. \"Aḥar\"), we follow the time the growth of the produce was completed. According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'aser Sheni 1:4), for vegetables we follow the actual harvesting, and not the time they finished growing. And so is the halakha (SA YD 331:126).<br>There are exceptions to the rule, namely <b>rice, millet and sesame</b>: although they are included among grain and legumes, since the time they reach a third is not uniform, it was established that we follow the harvesting for them (Rosh Ha-shana 14a; Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser Sheni 1:8). In the Rambam there, further details of these laws are explained.<br>Citrus fruits have the law of other trees, and so wrote Maran Ha-Rav Kook (Shabbat Ha-aretz, Kuntres Aḥaron 18) and Minḥat Shlomo (on Mishnayot Shevi'it 4:9). See Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it 3, nn. 6-7."}]},{"slug":"17-08-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-06/","title_he":"ו – גמר מלאכה","title_en":"Gemar Melakha","body_en":"<p>Even though the produce was picked after it reached onat ha-ma'asrot, as long as its processing has not been completed (\"nigmera melakhtam\"), one should not separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it, because it has not yet reached its choicest stage. In addition, only after \"gemar melakha\" (completion of processing) is it possible to calculate properly what must be separated for the tithes.<sup>10</sup></p><p>Gemar melakha is the completion of the work of gathering the produce from the field and preparing it for bringing home or for sale. For produce that is usually cleaned in the field, gemar melakha is after it is cleaned and its gathering is completed. When one intends to make wine from the grapes and oil from the olives, gemar melakha is upon completing the preparation of the wine and the oil; when one intends to eat the grapes fresh, gemar melakha is upon completing their gathering, and if as raisins – when they become raisins; and when one intends to eat the olives pickled, gemar melakha is at the end of their pickling. Gemar melakha for wheat is the \"miru'aḥ\" (smoothing of the pile), that is, after the stalks have been threshed to separate the grain kernels from them, and they have been winnowed in the wind to remove the chaff, and the remaining waste has been sorted out, and the wheat kernels have been properly set down in their place.</p><p>Although it is forbidden to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce that has not reached gemar melakha, after the fact, if one separated, he has fulfilled his obligation. Sometimes there is doubt as to when gemar melakha is, for example with produce that is usually gathered in the field into crates and afterwards undergoes cleaning, sorting and packing in the packing house in preparation for marketing. Ab initio one should resolve the doubt and wait until the packing is completed; but if there is a need to separate terumot u-ma'asrot earlier – because afterwards they may forget to separate from it, or afterwards it will be packed in a way that makes it difficult to separate from it, or perhaps the workers will take some of it home without separating terumot u-ma'asrot – it is proper to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it after completing the first stage of gathering.<sup>11</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"It is stated (Bamidbar 18:27): \"And your teruma shall be reckoned to you as though it were the grain of the threshing floor\" – grain from the threshing floor is the wheat kernels after they have been separated from the stalks and the chaff (Yerushalmi Terumot, end of chapter 1). And it is also stated: \"The tithe of your grain, your wine and your oil\" (Devarim 12:17; 14:23; 18:4). Tirosh is wine and yitzhar is oil; thus terumot u-ma'asrot are separated after the preparation of the produce has been completed. (See Yerushalmi Terumot 1:2, in the words of Rabbi Ḥananya and Rabbi Mana, from which come the two reasons I wrote.)"},{"n":11,"en":"The Rash and the Rosh wrote (Terumot 1:10) that it is implied by the Torah that ab initio one should not separate before gemar melakha; even so, after the fact what he did is done, because the Sages learned from the verse that if one tithes early from the stalks, the tithe he separated is obligated in terumat ma'aser but not in teruma (Yerushalmi Ḥalla 1:3; Bavli Pesaḥim 35b); thus by Torah law, after the fact one can separate terumot u-ma'asrot before gemar melakha. And some say that the law that ab initio one must separate from completed produce is rabbinic (Levush YD 331:54; Shakh 80).<br>Ab initio it is proper to separate upon completing all the gathering, at the point closest to bringing it home or to marketing. However, when there is a need to separate terumot u-ma'asrot earlier, one may separate after the completion of the first stage of gathering, and there are four reasons for this: <b>a)</b> If this rescues from the prohibition of eating tevel, it is possible that just as they permitted separating not from produce in proximity (min ha-mukaf), so too it is permitted before gemar melakha (Minḥat Shlomo 1:57:2). <b>b)</b> Perhaps this is already their gemar melakha, for at this stage they are already ready to be brought home or sold to greengrocers. <b>c)</b> Some say that for produce whose gemar melakha is only packing or light cleaning, it is permitted to separate terumot u-ma'asrot before gemar melakha (Or Same'aḥ, Hilkhot Terumot 5:4), because the kohanim and Levites suffer no loss from this. <b>d)</b> According to my teacher and rabbi, Rav Yisraeli, the basis of the prohibition of separating before gemar melakha is that the state of the produce before gemar melakha is considered \"inferior\" relative to its state after gemar melakha, and one may not separate from the inferior on behalf of the superior; and since today everything is inferior for the kohanim, it is permitted ab initio to separate before gemar melakha (Ha-Torah Ve-ha-aretz vol. 2, p. 299, and so it is implied by Arukh Ha-shulḥan, Zera'im 66:7). And even regarding ma'aser rishon and ma'aser ani, people customarily give their monetary value, and one can give the value of packed produce. Regarding one who wishes to eat them, it is obvious that it will be permitted to separate terumot u-ma'asrot before gemar melakha, for it is inconceivable that the Sages would forbid eating them in a fixed manner and also forbid separating terumot u-ma'asrot from them (Minḥat Shlomo 1:56:1).<br>The details of the laws of gemar melakha are explained in Mishna Ma'asrot 1:5-8; Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 3:8-16; Mishpetei Eretz 7:1-13; Kedushat Ha-aretz 7:21-50. Most of the problems are resolved by what I wrote above."}]},{"slug":"17-08-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-07/","title_he":"ז – דין הפירות בשלבי קביעתם למעשר","title_en":"The Law of Produce in the Stages of Becoming Fixed for Tithes","body_en":"<p>By Torah law, only after the processing of the produce is completed and it becomes fixed for tithes by being brought into the house is one obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it. As it is stated (Devarim 26:13): \"I have removed the holy things from the house\" – thus the Torah mitzva applies only to produce that has reached the house (Bava Metzi'a 88a). That is, although it is permitted ab initio to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce whose processing has been completed, the Torah prohibition of eating from it without separating terumot u-ma'asrot begins only from the time it is brought into the house.</p><p>The Sages added a safeguard to the Torah and established two halakhot:</p><p>The first: already from the time the produce has been picked, even though it has not yet become fixed for tithes, it is forbidden to eat from it in a fixed manner (akhilat keva), and it is forbidden to sow it in the ground. Only eating from it casually (arai), or feeding it to animals in a fixed manner, is permitted before the produce has become fixed for tithes (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 3:1-2; 20; 6:3).</p><p>The second: that the produce becomes fixed for tithes even before it is brought into the house. For if the owner of the field intends to sell it, immediately after its gemar melakha it becomes fixed for tithes, and it will be forbidden to eat from it even casually as long as terumot u-ma'asrot have not been separated from it. And even though in practice the produce has not yet reached the places where it will be sold, since its processing has already been completed, and if a buyer comes they will sell to him, it has become fixed for tithes. But if even when a buyer comes they will not sell to him, it is permitted to eat from it casually until it reaches the place where they intend to sell it, and it becomes fixed for tithes (Mishna Ma'asrot 2:3, Rash, Ra'av, Rash Sirilio; Yerushalmi Ma'asrot 1:4).</p><p>And if the owner of the produce intends to bring it to his house for eating, the Sages enacted that even before he brings it into his house, it becomes fixed for tithes through one of six additional things, and it will be forbidden to eat from it even casually without first separating terumot u-ma'asrot from it. These are the six things: 1) bringing it into a guarded courtyard; 2) mekaḥ – selling it; 3) cooking;<br>4) salting or pickling; 5) separating teruma gedola from it; 6) Shabbat – with the onset of Shabbat, produce designated for eating on Shabbat becomes fixed for tithes (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 3:3; 4:1-2).</p><p>One who is in doubt whether his eating is fixed or casual, or whether the produce has become fixed for tithes, should separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it with a blessing and thereby resolve the doubt.<sup>12</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"It is difficult to define what casual eating is, which is permitted before the produce has become fixed for tithes. Simply, it means a quick snack not within the framework of a meal. Similarly, it is explained in the Yerushalmi (Ma'asrot 4:1) that whatever is eaten within a framework of reclining and drinking wine is considered eaten in a fixed manner. Similarly, the Ra'avad wrote (Hilkhot Sekhirut 12:10) that if one ate bread, all the produce eaten within that same framework is considered eaten in a fixed manner. And the Yerushalmi there remains in doubt about the law of produce that one intended to eat while reclining with wine and in the end decided to eat without reclining.<br>From the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'aser 3:19), based on the Mishna, an additional rule emerges: if one is engaged in preparing the produce for eating, even not within the framework of a meal, it becomes fixed for tithes. For they said in the Mishna (Ma'asrot 4:5): \"One who husks barley may husk one at a time and eat, but if he husked and put them into his hand, he is obligated. One who rubs ears of wheat may sift from hand to hand and eat, but if he sifted and put them into his lap, he is obligated.\" According to Rashi and Meiri (Beitza 13b), one who gathers even two grains of wheat in his hand is considered to be eating them in a fixed manner. And this seems to be how the Rambam explained it. However, one can explain that the Mishna deals with one who husked or rubbed inside his house or his guarded courtyard, where any produce whose processing is completed immediately becomes fixed for tithes, and it is forbidden to eat from it even casually (as explained in the next halakha). But outside the guarded courtyard, eating wheat and barley in this way is considered casual. And some wrote that this is the Rambam's opinion (Sha'arei Tzedek 6, Binat Adam 1; Kerem Tziyon 13, p. 179). In addition, according to Tosafot, Rashba and Ritva (Beitza 13b), one who rubs and husks is considered to be completing the processing of the produce, but this is not considered fixed eating. And this is also the plain meaning of the sugya, that only one who eats in a fixed manner, such as eating that requires a sukka, is obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot. And since we are dealing with a rabbinic law, since the produce has not yet entered his house, and in addition, according to most poskim the law of terumot u-ma'asrot nowadays is rabbinic, one may rely on the opinion of the lenient, that whenever the eating is not fixed and not within a fixed framework, it is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot. One who wishes to be meticulous, whenever there is a doubt that perhaps his eating is fixed according to one of the opinions, should separate terumot u-ma'asrot with a blessing. One who takes a little produce from the orchard or the packing house before the packing is completed, in order to eat it casually in his car, is not obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it, for the car is not like a house.<br><b>Bringing into the house in an unusual way:</b> If a person brought produce whose processing was completed into his house through the window, it does not become fixed for tithes, and it is still permitted to eat from it casually. Rabbi Yehuda son of Rabbi Ila'i said: The later generations are not like the earlier generations. The earlier generations made their Torah fixed and their work casual, and they would bring in their produce through the door in order to become obligated in tithes, and they merited success in their Torah and in their work. The later generations made their work fixed and their Torah casual, and they would bring in their produce through the windows in order to eat from it casually without becoming obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, and neither their Torah nor their work endured in their hands (Berakhot 35b).<br>If a person brought into his house produce whose processing had not been completed, such as ears of grain, or even grain kernels with their chaff, he may eat from them casually until their processing is completed. After he separates them from the chaff, their processing is completed and they become fixed for tithes (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 3:6). Similarly, the opinion of the Ramban, Ritva and Ran (Bava Metzi'a 88b) is that anything from which a pile is made on the threshing floor does not require \"seeing the face of the house\" in order to become obligated in tithes, and therefore after its processing is completed, even when this is done in the house, it becomes obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. According to Rashi and Tosafot (Avoda Zara 41b), even after their processing is completed it is permitted to eat from them casually, for since he did not bring them into the house after their processing was completed, they do not become fixed for tithes; only when he wishes to eat them in a fixed manner will he be obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from them. And according to Rabbeinu Efrayim and the Rashba, since he brought them into his house before their processing was completed, they are exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot forever, and even after their processing is completed it will be permitted to eat from them in a fixed manner."}]},{"slug":"17-08-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-08/","title_he":"ח – פירות הגדלים בחצר","title_en":"Produce Growing in a Courtyard","body_en":"<p>Produce that grows in a guarded courtyard, which no stranger may enter, may be eaten casually only before its gemar melakha, that is, before it is gathered – for example, one grape or one fig, which are eaten in one go. But if he picks two grapes or two figs, or a large fruit that is not eaten in one go, such as an apple or a large fig, already in picking them they are gathered and their processing is completed, and they become obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, for this is the manner of gathering them in a courtyard. And since they are inside a guarded courtyard, which fixes them for tithes, they are forbidden even in casual eating before he separates terumot u-ma'asrot from them (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 4:15; 17; Radbaz there 18).</p><p>If he climbed a tree and picked many fruits there in a way that completed their processing, as long as he is on the tree and has not come down to the ground of the guarded courtyard, the produce has not yet become fixed for tithes, and since his eating on the tree is casual eating, he may eat from it without separating terumot u-ma'asrot (Rambam there).</p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-08-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-09/","title_he":"ט – מקום החיוב בארץ ישראל","title_en":"The Place of the Obligation in the Land of Israel","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of terumot u-ma'asrot is one of the mitzvot dependent on the Land, for everything that grows within the territory of the Land of Israel, including the eastern side of the Jordan and Syria, is included in the mitzva. However, when the majority of the Jewish people dwell in the Land, the obligation of the mitzva is from the Torah, and when the majority of the people are not in the Land, as in our situation today, the obligation of the mitzva is rabbinic. And some hold that in places settled by those who came up from Babylonia in the days of the Second Temple, even when the majority of the people is not in the Land, the obligation is from the Torah (below 12:11-12; Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it Ve-yovel 11:4 explains who is included in the count of the Jewish people for the law of the majority).</p><p>All the territories under the rule of the State of Israel are obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot with a blessing, and one may separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce growing in one place on behalf of produce growing in another place. However, it is proper not to separate from produce growing in territories of the Land of Israel under foreign rule, such as Jordan, Syria, Lebanon and Egypt, on behalf of produce growing in the State of Israel, and vice versa, because some hold that in the territories under Israeli rule the basis of the mitzva is from the Torah, and in the territories outside the borders of the State the basis of the mitzva is rabbinic. When the majority of Israel dwells in the Land, the obligation of the mitzva will be from the Torah in all the territories under Israeli rule (this topic is explained at length below 12:17).</p><p>Produce of the Land of Israel that is exported abroad – if its processing was completed in the Land, it is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, and if its processing was completed abroad, it is exempt. And if at the time of picking he intended to export it abroad, even if its processing was completed in the Land, some say it is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Maharsham), and some are stringent (Ḥazon Ish, Igrot Moshe). Since the law is rabbinic, those who wish to be lenient may do so.<sup>13</sup></p><p>Produce from abroad that entered the Land – if its processing was completed abroad, it is exempt, and if its processing was completed in the Land, it is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot by rabbinic law (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 1:22). Several Aḥaronim wrote that all this is on condition that the produce grew in a Jew's field, but produce that grew abroad in a gentile's field, even if its processing was completed in the Land, is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Aḥi'ezer, Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach). And although some are stringent (Ḥazon Ish), since the law is rabbinic, and so it seems, the halakha follows the lenient view.<sup>14</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Terumot 1:22) that produce of the Land of Israel that is exported abroad is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, and according to the Ra'avad it is obligated by rabbinic law even if its processing will be completed abroad. The Aḥaronim wrote that the halakha follows the Rambam, but they disagreed about his opinion. According to the Baḥ and Minḥat Ḥinukh, even if its processing was completed in the Land it is exempt, and according to the Radbaz, Mishneh La-melekh and most Aḥaronim, if its processing was completed in the Land it is obligated. And the Maharsham wrote (1:72) that even according to those who are stringent in interpreting the Rambam, they agree that if its processing was completed in the Land with the intention of exporting it abroad, it is exempt. And so wrote Mishpetei Uziel 6, YD 98. Admittedly, the Aḥi'ezer, Ḥazon Ish (Demai 15:4) and Igrot Moshe (YD 3:127) were stringent. However, since when produce is exported for sale the law is rabbinic, those who wish to be lenient may do so. All the more so, there is no need to separate terumot u-ma'asrot abroad from produce brought from the Land when there is doubt whether terumot u-ma'asrot were separated from it. And so wrote Rav Isser Zalman Meltzer, Rav Rosenthal and Yabi'a Omer 10, YD 46."},{"n":14,"en":"The Aḥi'ezer (2:39:6) and Ma'adanei Eretz (Terumot 1:22:9) exempted gentiles' produce that grew abroad, even if its processing was completed by a Jew in the Land, because all agree that a gentile has ownership (kinyan) of his land abroad. However, the Ḥazon Ish (Shevi'it 2:1) was stringent, and Ha-ma'aser Ve-ha-teruma (3, n. 29) inclined to this. And it seems that the halakha follows the lenient view, all the more so since the law is rabbinic, and in a doubt concerning a rabbinic law one is lenient."}]},{"slug":"17-08-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-10/","title_he":"י – פירות נוכרי","title_en":"Produce of a Gentile","body_en":"<p>A gentile who has land in the Land of Israel, and grew produce on it, and picked and gathered it – the produce is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Bekhorot 11b; Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 1:11). And if he sold the produce to a Jew before its gemar melakha, and the Jew completed the work of gathering it, the produce is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. For the halakha is that a gentile's ownership does not remove his land from the mitzvot; therefore, even though the produce grew in a gentile's land, since it was in a Jew's possession at its gemar melakha, which is the stage at which it becomes obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, it is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. However, regarding the giving of ma'aser rishon and ma'aser ani, one gives only according to the percentage of the produce that grew in the Jew's possession, as explained in the note.<sup>15</sup></p><p>Produce of a Jew's field that gentile workers picked and whose gathering they completed is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot by rabbinic law. Produce of a gentile's field that Jewish workers picked and whose gathering they completed – according to most poskim, one is obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot; but since some are lenient, one should separate terumot u-ma'asrot without a blessing.<sup>16</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"The Tanna'im disagreed (Yerushalmi Demai 5:8) whether a gentile has ownership in the Land of Israel that removes the produce growing on his land from the mitzvot dependent on the Land, such as terumot u-ma'asrot. According to Rabbi Meir, he has no ownership, that is, his purchase of the land does not remove it from the mitzvot; and according to Rabbi Yehuda and Rabbi Shimon, he has ownership, so that his ownership removes it from the mitzvot. Likewise in the Bavli (Gittin 47a) the Amora'im disagreed about this: according to Rabba he has no ownership, and according to Rabbi Elazar he has ownership. In the Yerushalmi it is explicitly ruled that a gentile has ownership that removes the land from the obligation of the mitzvot. And so wrote the Ra'av (Pe'a 4:9; Demai 5:9). And this is also the opinion of Rabbeinu Ḥananel and the Arukh. However, the Bavli implies that he has no ownership. There are even opinions in a baraita (Menaḥot 66b) that even if the gemar melakha of the produce was done by the gentile, the produce is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. And there were poskim who ruled so (Ra'avad, Hilkhot Terumot 1:13). However, according to the halakha, a gentile's ownership does not remove the mitzva of terumot u-ma'asrot on condition that the gemar melakha was done by a Jew; but if the gemar melakha was done by a gentile, the produce is exempt, as Rabba said (Gittin 47a). And so ruled the Rambam (Hilkhot Terumot 1:10-11), and in Tzefat they even placed under ban whoever separated terumot u-ma'asrot from a gentile's produce whose processing was also completed by a gentile (Kesef Mishneh there). Rather, the meaning is that a gentile has no ownership in the Land of Israel to remove the obligation of terumot u-ma'asrot if the gemar melakha is done by a Jew; for if the gemar melakha was done by a Jew, he must separate terumot u-ma'asrot with a blessing. And one should not say that since some hold that a gentile has ownership that removes the mitzva, because of the doubt one should separate without a blessing, because the custom is to recite the blessing (Yabi'a Omer 5, YD 28:1).<br>As stated, since the gemar melakha was done by a Jew, the produce is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot; however, regarding the giving of the gifts, the law is different. He should separate teruma gedola and ma'aser sheni as usual, but ma'aser rishon and ma'aser ani he should separate and not give to the Levite and the poor person, for since the Jew bought the produce from a gentile, he can say to the Levite and the poor person: \"I come by the right of a person from whom you cannot take anything; therefore, by buying the produce before gemar melakha and becoming obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, I did not become obligated to give you gifts that are monetary.\" And since he need not give ma'aser rishon, he also need not give terumat ma'aser to the kohen free of charge; rather, he separates terumat ma'aser and sells it to the kohen at its value (Bekhorot 11b). And if the Jew bought the produce while it was still growing on the trees – if it had not yet reached onat ha-ma'asrot, he must separate terumot u-ma'asrot from it as usual; and if he bought it after it reached onat ha-ma'asrot, he separates teruma gedola and ma'aser sheni as usual, and ma'aser rishon and ma'aser ani he separates as usual but gives only according to the percentages that grew in his possession. For if half the weight of the produce grew in his possession, he gives half of the ma'aser rishon to the Levite and half of the ma'aser ani to the poor person, and half of the terumat ma'aser he gives to the kohen free of charge, and half he sells to him (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 1:11-12).<br>A Jew who bought from a gentile grapes that were harvested in order to make wine, and made wine from them – since their gemar melakha was done by a Jew, he must separate terumot u-ma'asrot from them, as explained above. And if the gentile grew them for eating and completed their gathering, and afterwards the Jew bought them from him in order to make wine from them, some say he must separate terumot u-ma'asrot (Radbaz and Shelah), but according to most poskim there is no need to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from them (Mabit, Rashdam, Maharash Garmizan, Admat Kodesh 1, YD 21)."},{"n":16,"en":"Regarding produce of a Jew's field whose processing a gentile worker completed, the Meiri wrote (Gittin 47a) that it is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot by Torah law, because we follow the owner of the field. According to the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim, since the worker is a gentile, the obligation is only rabbinic (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 1:13; Ramban, Rashba and Ritva, Kiddushin 41b; Or Zaru'a, Tosafot Rid, Ra'avya, Shelah, and others).<br>Regarding produce of a gentile's field whose processing a Jewish worker completed, several Rishonim wrote that we follow the owner of the field and the produce is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Tosafot Ha-Rosh and Tur; and so it appears from the words of the Meiri above). And the Ḥazon Ish wrote (Demai 12:19) that one may rely on them and exempt the produce from terumot u-ma'asrot. However, in practice, since we learned regarding a Jew's field that according to the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim we follow the worker, one must separate terumot u-ma'asrot (Har Tzvi, Zera'im 1:17; Minḥat Shlomo 1:37). But since some are lenient, one should separate without a blessing (Rav Yehuda Amiḥai, Ha-Torah Ve-ha-aretz 8).<br>The owner of the field must take care not to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce picked by a gentile on behalf of produce picked by a Jewish worker, and vice versa (Mishpat Kohen 33). However, after the fact, if there is no way to rectify the matter, since the law of terumot u-ma'asrot nowadays is rabbinic, it is permitted to separate from one on behalf of the other, relying on those who hold that we follow the owner of the field (and so wrote the Ḥazon Ish there)."}]},{"slug":"17-08-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-11/","title_he":"יא – עוד מדיני נוכרי","title_en":"More Laws Concerning a Gentile","body_en":"<p>A Jew who sold his produce to a gentile before gemar melakha – even though by Torah law the produce is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot, the Sages enacted that it be obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. This is because of \"ba'alei kisim,\" that is, wealthy people, who, in order to evade giving terumot u-ma'asrot, would sell their produce to a gentile before gemar melakha. To prevent them from doing so, the Sages enacted that even if the gentile completes the work of gathering it, the produce will be obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. Only if the Jew sells his produce to a gentile while it is still on the trees, before it reaches onat ha-ma'asrot, that is, before it is fit to be eaten in a pressing situation, and it remains in the gentile's possession until after gemar melakha, will the produce be exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Menaḥot 67a, Rashi and Rash; Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 1:13, Radbaz).</p><p>When a Jew and a gentile are partners in a field, the produce is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot. To resolve the doubt, it is good for them to divide the field in advance, so that the gentile's part will be exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot and the Jew's part will be obligated. After the fact, if they did not do so, they should divide the produce between them, and the Jew should separate from his share (SA 331:11, and see the Harḥavot).</p><p>A gentile is exempt from separating terumot u-ma'asrot from produce that grew in his field in the Land of Israel, and he also cannot separate terumot u-ma'asrot on behalf of a Jew. But if he wishes, he may volunteer to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from his produce (Mishna Terumot 3:9, Yerushalmi 3:5). Some say this is a rabbinic enactment (Rambam, Hilkhot Terumot 4:15; SA 331:44). And some say that this is the law by Torah law, for just as a gentile can consecrate a sacrifice, so he can separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce he grew in the Land of Israel (Rashi, Tosafot; see Har Tzvi OḤ 2:2).</p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-08-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-12/","title_he":"יב – מקומות שאינם שדה","title_en":"Places That Are Not a Field","body_en":"<p>By Torah law, only what grows in the ground is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot, and what grows in a perforated pot is also considered to grow in the ground. But what grows in a pot without a hole, or on substrates that separate it from the ground, is exempt. And the Sages obligated it in terumot u-ma'asrot with a blessing (Yoma 83b).</p><p>What grows inside a house, even though its floor is earth, is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (Ra'avad). According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'aser 1:10), although by Torah law it is indeed exempt, by rabbinic law it is obligated; therefore one should separate terumot u-ma'asrot without a blessing.</p><p>One is obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot from produce that grows in greenhouses.<sup>17</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":17,"en":"They said in the Yerushalmi (Orla 1:2), regarding a tree that grows in a house, that it is exempt from terumot u-ma'asrot (even though its floor is earth), because only what grows in a field is obligated, as it is stated (Devarim 14:22): \"You shall surely tithe all the produce of your seed that the field brings forth year by year.\" But regarding Shevi'it the Yerushalmi remains in doubt. According to the Rambam, the words of the Yerushalmi refer only to the Torah obligation, but by rabbinic law one must separate also from what grows in a house (Hilkhot Ma'aser 1:10). According to the Ra'avad, it is exempt even by rabbinic law. Therefore, in practice, one should separate without a blessing. Regarding greenhouses, according to the Rambam, who holds that even what grows in a house is obligated, it is obvious that what grows in greenhouses is also obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot with a blessing; and some say that according to the Ra'avad they are exempt, and therefore in practice they rule not to recite a blessing. However, it seems simple that regarding greenhouses even the Ra'avad would agree, for the exemption in a house is because it is not the way to grow produce in a house; but in a greenhouse, where it is customary to grow, there is an obligation of terumot u-ma'asrot. Therefore one must separate terumot u-ma'asrot with a blessing from produce growing in a greenhouse, and give ma'aser rishon to a Levite and ma'aser ani to a poor person.<br><b>Hydroponic produce:</b> Some say it is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot like a field (Har Tzvi, Zera'im 2:31), and some say that since there is no soil there it is exempt (based on Neḥpa Ba-kesef 1, YD 5; Sefer Ha-shemita vol. 2, 8:2, p. 97). In practice, one should separate terumot u-ma'asrot without a blessing, and so wrote Mishpetei Eretz 1:19; Kedushat Ha-aretz 1:53.<br><b>Regarding Shevi'it</b>, I wrote in Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it 2, n. 13, regarding what grows in a house, that since Shevi'it nowadays is rabbinic, it is permitted to grow vegetables in a house (Pe'at Ha-shulḥan 20, n. 52). Regarding greenhouses the Aḥaronim disagreed: some say their law is like that of a house, and since Shevi'it nowadays is rabbinic, it is permitted to grow vegetables in a greenhouse (Yabi'a Omer 9, YD 31; Minḥat Shlomo 3:158:7). And some say that since it is customary to grow vegetables in a greenhouse, its law is like that of a field, in which work is forbidden in the seventh year (Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv), and it is obligated in terumot u-ma'asrot with a blessing (Rav Nissim Karelitz). And so it seems, that the law of greenhouses is like that of a field. However, I wrote that if the vegetables are grown in a pot without a hole, since this is a double doubt concerning rabbinic laws, those who wish to be lenient may do so. But regarding terumot u-ma'asrot one should not be lenient in such a case (even though in principle the law of Shevi'it is more severe than the law of terumot u-ma'asrot, as explained in the Yerushalmi), because the law of Shevi'it nowadays is more lenient than the law of terumot u-ma'asrot. For some hold that today one is obligated to separate terumot u-ma'asrot by Torah law (Rashi and Ra'avad), and according to many Rishonim, after the establishment of the State the obligation returned to being from the Torah (Rashba and those who agree with him, below 12:17, n. 18). And according to the other poskim, it is a full rabbinic obligation (below 12:11). Whereas regarding Shevi'it, according to most poskim it is a rabbinic obligation, and some Rishonim hold that it is merely a custom of piety. Furthermore, the doubt about the years weakens the obligation of Shevi'it more than the obligation of terumot u-ma'asrot (Peninei Halakha: Shevi'it 5:6-7). Furthermore, even according to the central view of the Rambam, that the law of both is rabbinic, Shevi'it is further from us, for terumot u-ma'asrot are rabbinic only because the majority of its inhabitants are not upon it, whereas Shevi'it is rabbinic because the majority of each and every tribe is not dwelling in its inheritance (ibid. 5:3)."}]},{"slug":"17-08-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-08-13/","title_he":"יג – דמאי ושאר ספקות","title_en":"Demai and Other Doubts","body_en":"<p>In the language of the Sages, \"tevel\" is produce from which terumot u-ma'asrot have not been separated, and the meaning of \"tevel\" is \"tov-lo\" (\"not good\"), that is, this produce is not yet good for eating. \"Demai\" is produce about which there is doubt whether terumot u-ma'asrot were separated from it. \"Demai\" is an Aramaic word whose translation is \"What is this?\" That is, there is a question about this produce: is it tithed?</p><p>In the time of the Sages, most amei ha-aretz would separate terumot u-ma'asrot, but since some of them did not separate them properly, the Sages enacted that the produce of amei ha-aretz would have the status of demai: teruma gedola is not separated from it, because its separation was widespread among the amei ha-aretz, but ma'aser rishon, terumat ma'aser, and ma'aser sheni or ani are separated. However, in practice, even though they separated the ma'aser rishon and ma'aser ani produce by designating a place, the Sages did not obligate giving them to the Levite and the poor person. For they are monetary obligations, to which the rule applies: \"The burden of proof is on the one who seeks to extract from his fellow\"; and unless the Levite or the poor person proves that tithes were indeed not separated from this produce, they need not be given. But from the ma'aser rishon one must separate terumat ma'aser and give it to the kohen, since it has sanctity and an Israelite is forbidden to eat it. Likewise regarding ma'aser sheni, since it has sanctity, one must separate it and eat it in Jerusalem in purity. One does not recite a blessing on the separation of terumot u-ma'asrot and the redemption of ma'aser sheni from demai produce, according to the well-known rule: \"In a doubt about blessings, one is lenient\" (see Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser 9:1-4). And if one redeems ma'aser sheni of demai, there is no need to add a fifth (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'aser Sheni 5:4).</p><p>Today, when a doubt arises about produce whether it is tithed, the doubt also concerns teruma gedola, since amei ha-aretz do not separate it either. Therefore one should separate all the terumot u-ma'asrot without a blessing; however, as stated, the monetary obligations – ma'aser rishon and ma'aser ani – need not be given to the Levite and the poor person, whereas the sanctified terumot must, because of the doubt, be placed wrapped in the trash bin, and the ma'aser sheni must be redeemed on a peruta.</p><p>Whenever a person is in doubt whether the produce before him belongs to a ma'aser sheni year or a ma'aser ani year, he should separate both tithes because of the doubt and say: \"If it is obligated in ani, let it be ma'aser ani, and if in sheni, let it be sheni.\" He should redeem the ma'aser sheni on a peruta and give the ma'aser ani to a poor person. And one who wishes to be lenient may separate ma'aser sheni alone.<sup>18</sup></p><p class=\"ph-media\"><i>[Video in the original Hebrew page]</i></p>","notes_en":[{"n":18,"en":"In Mishna Makhshirin 2:11 it is explained that when produce of a ma'aser sheni year became mixed with produce of a ma'aser ani year, we follow the majority, and if they are \"half and half – one is stringent.\" According to many, this means that he should separate two tithes; that is, he should separate ma'aser sheni and redeem it with money, which he takes up to Jerusalem, and give the redeemed produce to a poor person (Rash, Ra'avad, Rosh, Ra'av, Gra). According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'aser Sheni 1:11), it means that he should separate ma'aser sheni, which is more severe since it has sanctity. And so wrote SA (331:128). Since according to most poskim one should separate two tithes, this is the proper practice. However, since the Aḥaronim ruled that the law of terumot u-ma'asrot nowadays is rabbinic, one who wishes to be lenient and separate ma'aser sheni alone may do so, for he has something to rely on."}]}]});
